WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.975
Session security depends on the full lifecycle: creation, login, use, renewal, expiration, logout, and invalidation.

2
00:00:06.975 --> 00:00:16.276
A session may exist before login for anonymous state such as a shopping cart, then become authenticated after successful sign-in.

3
00:00:16.276 --> 00:00:19.996
That transition is security-sensitive.

4
00:00:19.996 --> 00:00:30.692
The application should avoid carrying risky assumptions across the boundary and should renew or rotate session state when authentication or privilege level changes.

5
00:00:30.692 --> 00:00:34.412
Timeouts define how long a session remains useful.

6
00:00:34.412 --> 00:00:39.528
An idle timeout ends a session after a period of inactivity.

7
00:00:39.528 --> 00:00:45.573
An absolute timeout ends a session after a maximum lifetime regardless of activity.

8
00:00:45.573 --> 00:00:51.618
Both controls reduce exposure from unattended browsers, forgotten devices, and stale authenticated state.

9
00:00:51.618 --> 00:00:57.199
The right values depend on application risk, user workflow, and regulatory expectations.

10
00:00:57.199 --> 00:01:03.244
High-risk administrative functions often need shorter lifetimes or step-up verification than low-risk browsing.

11
00:01:03.244 --> 00:01:11.150
Logout and invalidation must be real server-side events, not just removal of a local user interface indicator.

12
00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:24.636
When a user logs out, changes a password, loses privileges, enables MFA, disables an account, or reports suspicious activity, existing sessions may need to be ended, renewed, or reviewed.

13
00:01:24.636 --> 00:01:28.356
Concurrent sessions require policy decisions too.

14
00:01:28.356 --> 00:01:33.471
Some applications allow many active devices; others restrict or display them.

15
00:01:33.471 --> 00:01:41.377
Lifecycle management makes session state responsive to security events rather than treating login as a one-time decision.
